stream
BT
/F2 20 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 762 Tm (Does Cycle Counting Reduce Inventory Error? A) Tj
/F2 20 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 738 Tm (Review of the Evidence) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 717.5 Tm (Prepared by: Dr Imogen Ward, Logistics Research Group) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 698.5 Tm (Date: October 2026) Tj
/F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 669.0 Tm (Abstract) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 653.5 Tm (Warehouse operators have long relied on annual physical inventories to correct stock records.) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 639.5 Tm (Cycle counting, in which a small subset of items is counted on a rolling basis, is widely) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 625.5 Tm (recommended as an alternative. This review summarises 14 studies published between 2009) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 611.5 Tm (and 2025 to assess whether cycle counting measurably improves inventory record accuracy,) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 597.5 Tm (and under what conditions.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 568.0 Tm (Background) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 552.5 Tm (Inventory record accuracy \(IRA\) is the proportion of stock records that match physical quantity) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 538.5 Tm (within an agreed tolerance. Typical accuracy in manufacturing and distribution settings has been) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 524.5 Tm (reported at 60 to 75 percent, which is far below the 95 percent or higher that most planning) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 510.5 Tm (systems assume. Inaccurate records lead to stockouts, excess safety stock, and wasted picking) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 496.5 Tm (effort.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 467.0 Tm (Method) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 451.5 Tm (We searched four databases for studies reporting before and after accuracy for sites adopting) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 437.5 Tm (cycle counting. Studies were included if they measured accuracy at item level and reported the) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 423.5 Tm (counting frequency. Eleven studies were case studies and three were controlled trials. Quality) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 409.5 Tm (was assessed using a simple three point scale.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 380.0 Tm (Findings) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 364.5 Tm (Twelve of the 14 studies reported an improvement in IRA after cycle counting was introduced.) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 350.5 Tm (The average improvement was 19 percentage points, with a range from 4 to 33. Sites that) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 336.5 Tm (counted high-value items more often than low-value ones \(an ABC approach\) improved more than) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 322.5 Tm (sites using a uniform schedule.) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 303.5 Tm (Three factors appeared to explain most of the variation. First, sites that investigated the cause of) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 289.5 Tm (each discrepancy, rather than simply adjusting the record, improved faster. Second, blind) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 275.5 Tm (counts, where the counter is not shown the expected quantity, reduced confirmation bias. Third,) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 261.5 Tm (sites that triggered extra counts after exceptions, such as short picks, found errors sooner than) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 247.5 Tm (sites that relied on the schedule alone.) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 228.5 Tm (Two studies found no significant benefit. Both were in sites with very high staff turnover where) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 214.5 Tm (counters received little training.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 185.0 Tm (Limitations) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 169.5 Tm (Most studies were observational and self-reported. Publication bias is likely, since operators) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 155.5 Tm (with poor results may not publish them. Definitions of tolerance varied between studies, which) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 141.5 Tm (makes direct comparison difficult.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 112.0 Tm (Recommendations) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 96.5 Tm (Operators introducing cycle counting should:) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 74 77.5 Tm (- Classify items by value and count the highest-value group most often.) Tj
/F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 74 61.5 Tm (- Use blind counts by default.) Tj
ET
